The internals of `ca::heap` and `ca::arena`.

### Heap

Everything below is templated on the geometry `G` (`ca::is_heap_geometry`) of a
`ca::basic_heap`; `ca::heap`'s is 1 MiB spans and classes up to 128 KiB.

- `heap_classes.hpp`: up to 48 size classes, 16 to 128 bytes by 16, then four per doubling up to
  `G::LARGEST_CLASS`. Every class is a multiple of 16. `heap_class_of(size)` is a shift for small
  sizes and a `bit_width` above 128, always inlined: it sits on the allocation fast path.
- `heap_span.hpp`: a span is `G::SPAN_SIZE` bytes aligned to its size, serving one class from
  its start, its header in the last bytes, so `heap_span_of(block)` finds it by masking the
  block's address. The header's first cache line belongs to the owning heap (its free list, its
  use count); the second takes frees from other heaps, as an atomic list, or the full mark once
  the owner found the span exhausted, after which such frees go to the owner heap's `_pending`
  list instead, so a foreign thread never touches a span the owner may release. With
  `G::CHECK_DOUBLE_FREE`, a bit per 16 bytes, right below the header, marks the blocks in use
  (`heap_mark`, `heap_unmark`, which also traps on an address off a 16-byte boundary); frees from
  other heaps are checked when the owner collects them.
- `heap_state.hpp`: a heap, holding per class a queue of spans with room, and with
  `G::STATISTICS` its counters (blocks in use and spans per class, spare spans), written by the
  owner alone with relaxed stores. Contexts that have not allocated yet point at the heap's
  `empty_state()`, whose classes all hold the empty span, so the fast path has no null check: the
  first allocation takes the slow path, which builds a real heap and gives it to the binding.
  Heaps are never freed: a retired one (`ca::heap`: a thread's exit, through a thread key) goes to
  a pool, and the next new context adopts it with its spans.
- `heap_engine.hpp`: the slow paths, the pool and the large-block counters of one instantiation,
  internal to the translation unit that includes it and names the instantiation.
- `heap_traps.hpp`: the named traps of the heap's checks.

### Arena

`arena_state.hpp`: an arena's state lives in its first chunk, right after the `arena_chunk`
header (previous chunk, size), so it never moves and the `arena_allocator`s, which point to it,
stay valid when the `ca::arena` object itself is moved. Blocks are carved from `_top` up to
`_end` of the newest chunk; a request that does not fit starts a new chunk, or gets a chunk of
its own when larger than the chunk size.
